## Independent findings

The source delta is only `ProblemDescription.PDESC` and `SystemTransfer.TFTIME` 8.5→10.5. The accepted parent has 34 authored tables, 22 rows and 223 cells, with the four SystemTransfer key flags intact. The child export oracle preserves the accepted native export table ordering and 18 all-null placeholder rows: 34 tables, 40 rows and 407 cells. Declared-double data can be encoded as JSON integers or floats; schema, metadata, nulls, keys, and authored source types remain strict.

The actual accepted parent report, after only its known final-table trailing-comma repair in memory, agrees with the complete 4,044-cell parent oracle. Separately derived half-hour interval formulas check all 4,044 predicted child cells. The paired report difference is exactly five cells: `PDESC` and `NSYRY`/`NSYA` in each of the 10–10.5 and 10.5–11 bins. The in-unit integral remains 71.5; the ready integral 59.5. Other predeclared integrals remain equal across arms.

The proposed full log is anchored to the accepted parent's exact header and tail, parent-derived prefix, departure lines shifted from h10 to h10.5, and arrival lines shifted from h10.5 to h11. A source ready-list diagnostic is newly predicted at h10. Only the two declared optional diagnostic blocks may appear between departure and arrival, in their specified order, yielding exactly four permitted transcripts of 118–121 lines. The transfer request time is source-authored and not separately logged. The physical ledger holds two systems, moves system 0 from ready source to transit at departure and to ready target on arrival, and leaves the two missions at h8–10 and h20–22. Manual scope is limited to ready-system availability, authored TFTIME and BETWEEN transport TFDT; this is not a general priority or idle-transfer rule.

## Preserved reviewer failures and limits

The first reviewer audit, SHA-256 `06eae2b75b711543cef8fa92be87063ac6a355cb724adc77e789ae503782b959`, failed by assuming authored and native-export table orders match. The second, SHA-256 `3d3418aa163974960d05ebcc564d6e64b2623ccef0ad0af4b066b00e1c777092`, failed by requiring JSON int/float type identity for declared-double report cells. Those were reviewer-method errors, not contract failures. Their failure output was observed through decoded tool transcripts only; no original stdout/stderr bytes are claimed. The successful third and fourth auditors remain separate historical sources, SHA-256 `6690771ddaf00a0269bac1b766c1fa1618f4e3d73a1f26f3f406e53d36987227` and `ca2cc7bf07d425004eb8730e01b9787145fae2c5a157623064387393ed48fbaf`. The fifth auditor adds read-only Git blob checks for all 76 identities.

No child package, child raw observation, accepted checker rerun, SSH call or native call was used for this decision. Any child log outside the four declared transcripts, or any source, export, report, custody or physical-ledger discrepancy requires a fresh NO_GO; the contract must not be fitted to that observation.
